WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.100
-Oh, my gosh.
-Wow.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.367
-I'm wearing your suit.

00:00:03.367 --> 00:00:04.434
-Wait.

00:00:04.434 --> 00:00:05.868
[ Laughter ]

00:00:05.868 --> 00:00:07.534
You're wearing my suit?
-I'm wearing your suit,

00:00:07.534 --> 00:00:10.367
'cause I have a big
announcement, which is the show

00:00:10.367 --> 00:00:12.200
I've been touring with,
which you've seen, called

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.400
"The Old Man &amp; The Pool,"
and that Questlove

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.667
did the pre-show music for,
is going to Broadway.

00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:20.000
-Yes! Come on!

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.367
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:21.367 --> 00:00:23.767
It's a big announcement
right here.

00:00:23.767 --> 00:00:26.434
-I showed up -- I showed up --
I showed up today,

00:00:26.434 --> 00:00:28.067
and I'm wearing, like,
my corduroy shirt

00:00:28.067 --> 00:00:29.634
that I was doing with the game
and everything,

00:00:29.634 --> 00:00:31.167
and I was like,
I got to dress up.

00:00:31.167 --> 00:00:33.267
It's Lincoln Center,
this is like, legit.

00:00:33.267 --> 00:00:34.734
So I gotta look nice.

00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:37.100
So if I can wear this for the
opening night, it'd be great.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.567
-Yeah. You look -- you look
fantastic in my suit.

00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:42.100
-Thank you so much.
-That really is my suit?

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.567
-Yeah, well, it belongs to NBC.

00:00:43.567 --> 00:00:45.000
[ Laughter ]

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.234
-Yeah, I guess it does.
-I'm not bringing this home.

00:00:46.234 --> 00:00:48.434
-Wait, what theater?
Where are you -- this --

00:00:48.434 --> 00:00:50.033
-It's gonna be the Vivian
Beaumont Theater

00:00:50.033 --> 00:00:51.467
at Lincoln Center.
-Yeah.

00:00:51.467 --> 00:00:54.234
-Broadway Lincoln Center,
and it's starting

00:00:54.234 --> 00:00:56.767
October 28th through Christmas.

00:00:56.767 --> 00:00:58.868
-So if you're in New York City
during the holidays...

00:00:58.868 --> 00:01:01.100
-Perfect time.
-Dude, I'm so proud of you.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:02.434
-It's happening for you.
-It's exciting.

00:01:02.434 --> 00:01:05.133
-Yes!
-And you're so, honestly, like,

00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:07.534
it's so much of
"The Tonight Show" DNA

00:01:07.534 --> 00:01:09.601
because you've been
supportive of the show.

00:01:09.601 --> 00:01:11.200
You came on my podcast
working it out,

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:12.901
you've been to two
of the live shows,

00:01:12.901 --> 00:01:14.968
Questlove did the preshow music,
which is amazing.

00:01:14.968 --> 00:01:16.834
I listened -- I was
just in Los Angeles.

00:01:16.834 --> 00:01:18.968
I did the show for five weeks
at Marquee Performer,

00:01:18.968 --> 00:01:21.300
and I would listen to
Questlove's preshow music

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:22.534
every night, and it's amazing.

00:01:22.534 --> 00:01:25.501
It's got like "Sesame Street"
tracks in it.

00:01:25.501 --> 00:01:27.400
-I --
-It's got like The Cure

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.534
and like all these, like,
great, amazing tracks.

00:01:29.534 --> 00:01:31.701
-How'd you get Quest to --
to do that?

00:01:31.701 --> 00:01:34.767
-I watched master class
about deejaying.

00:01:34.767 --> 00:01:36.033
[ Laughter ]

00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:37.133
True story.

00:01:37.133 --> 00:01:38.601
I watched his master class
about deejaying,

00:01:38.601 --> 00:01:39.934
and it's so good.

00:01:39.934 --> 00:01:41.567
I highly recommend it.
-Yeah.

00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:44.667
-And then I was like I want this
to be before my show,

00:01:44.667 --> 00:01:46.200
like when people are coming in.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.200
And so I tried to do it myself,
and I was like,

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.934
"This is much harder
than he makes it seem

00:01:50.934 --> 00:01:52.234
in his master class."

00:01:52.234 --> 00:01:54.167
So I called and I said,
like, would you be willing

00:01:54.167 --> 00:01:56.033
to try this, and he did it
as a favor,

00:01:56.033 --> 00:01:58.000
and it's like
I'm behind grateful.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.234
It's just amazing.

00:01:59.234 --> 00:02:02.067
-Mike, I remember walking
through this with you.

00:02:02.067 --> 00:02:03.701
Like, yeah, I don't
if there's a show here,

00:02:03.701 --> 00:02:05.701
but I think there is
and I'm going to grow it

00:02:05.701 --> 00:02:08.467
and hopefully, you know, the
goal is to get it on Broadway.

00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:09.834
-Yeah.
And I was like, "Alright,"

00:02:09.834 --> 00:02:11.334
and I never mentioned it
to you ever again

00:02:11.334 --> 00:02:12.667
'cause I don't want
to jinx anything.

00:02:12.667 --> 00:02:14.334
-Yeah, and I appreciate that.

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:16.000
So, we mounted it
for the first time

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.267
with like the full set design
and everything in Los Angeles,

00:02:19.267 --> 00:02:23.200
and it's been the most
gratifying performing experience

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.968
in my life, because it's about
life and death and mortality

00:02:25.968 --> 00:02:28.234
and all these things that, like,
people don't want to talk about.

00:02:28.234 --> 00:02:30.434
But the things that people say

00:02:30.434 --> 00:02:32.000
when they come up to me
are so sweet.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.067
Like, this guy was like,
"I brought my 12-year-old son,

00:02:34.067 --> 00:02:35.434
and on the way home, like,

00:02:35.434 --> 00:02:37.400
we talked about the afterlife,"
you know what I mean?

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:39.901
And like, I had this woman
come up and say, like,

00:02:39.901 --> 00:02:42.968
"I lost my dad a few years ago,
and tonight I felt like I was

00:02:42.968 --> 00:02:44.934
watching the show with my dad."

00:02:44.934 --> 00:02:47.400
-Yeah. It's --
it's moving things.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.334
-I mean, the theater's haunted,
so take it with a grain of salt.

00:02:51.334 --> 00:02:53.100
-[ Laughs ] "The theatre's
haunted."

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.901
But there's so many lines
in there -- I just, I mean,

00:02:55.901 --> 00:02:58.200
you probably want to delete my
phone number from your contacts,

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.067
'cause I was like, "Alright,
no I love this line.

00:03:00.067 --> 00:03:01.200
Oh, no, and then
I love this line."

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.834
Then "this is my
favorite line."

00:03:02.834 --> 00:03:04.801
I don't want to give anything
away, but there's --

00:03:04.801 --> 00:03:07.267
there's so many great lines
in this show.

00:03:07.267 --> 00:03:09.667
-Well, it's funny
'cause I loved it when you came,

00:03:09.667 --> 00:03:11.667
because your laugh is so --

00:03:11.667 --> 00:03:13.701
you're like such
a generous laugher.

00:03:13.701 --> 00:03:19.033
And -- and as a comedian -- as a
comedian, you just crave that.

00:03:19.033 --> 00:03:21.400
And then the funniest thing is,
when my friends would come,

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.801
typically like, the -- 'cause I
talk about having a lot of

00:03:24.801 --> 00:03:25.868
pre-existing conditions.

00:03:25.868 --> 00:03:27.334
I have a sleep walking disorder,

00:03:27.334 --> 00:03:30.133
I had cancer when I was 20,
like, a lot of things,

00:03:30.133 --> 00:03:32.234
and people come up to me
afterwards

00:03:32.234 --> 00:03:33.868
and are like, "Are you okay?"

00:03:33.868 --> 00:03:35.234
And --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:35.234 --> 00:03:37.667
For real, and I'm like -- and
after a while I'm like,

00:03:37.667 --> 00:03:39.200
"Am I?"
You know what I mean?

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:40.601
-No, you're fine.

00:03:40.601 --> 00:03:43.567
-But I came back to New York
after the Los Angeles trip

00:03:43.567 --> 00:03:46.901
and I was like, "I'm going
to go get a cystoscopy."

00:03:46.901 --> 00:03:48.934
This is 'cause I had bladder
cancer when I was 20,

00:03:48.934 --> 00:03:51.000
but it's funny because
I'm a hypochondriac,

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.000
and I think the funniest thing
that can happen

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.934
to a hypochondriac
is you get cancer.

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:57.067
You know what I mean?
'Cause you're like, "See?

00:03:57.067 --> 00:03:59.067
I told you!"
-No, no --

00:03:59.067 --> 00:04:00.801
-"Remember last week when
I thought I had Rickets?

00:04:00.801 --> 00:04:02.300
I was probably right
about that, too!"

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:03.467
-Yeah, you're --
-There's gonna be

00:04:03.467 --> 00:04:05.267
a lot of changes around here."

00:04:05.267 --> 00:04:07.067
-"Rickets."
-Um, no, but I was --

00:04:07.067 --> 00:04:09.400
I was lucky because
they took it --

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:11.067
when I was 20,
they took it out,

00:04:11.067 --> 00:04:12.934
but I have to go for this thing
called a cystoscopy,

00:04:12.934 --> 00:04:15.400
and so they take a -- a rod --
they, like,

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.734
take a camera and they
stick it through

00:04:17.734 --> 00:04:19.367
your penis to look and --
[ Audience ohs ]

00:04:19.367 --> 00:04:21.267
No, I know what you're thinking,
"Mike, a camera

00:04:21.267 --> 00:04:23.167
can't fit through a penis."

00:04:23.167 --> 00:04:24.868
Good news and bad news
on that front.

00:04:24.868 --> 00:04:27.100
[ Laughter ]

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:29.467
The good news is it can,
and the bad news

00:04:29.467 --> 00:04:31.400
is the same as the good news.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.767
[ Laughter ]

00:04:33.767 --> 00:04:34.934
[ Rim shot ]

00:04:34.934 --> 00:04:37.200
Thank you, Quest.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:38.467
But here's -- here's what
happened.

00:04:38.467 --> 00:04:40.167
-"The bad news is the same
as the good news."

00:04:40.167 --> 00:04:44.400
-But here's what happened --
I went for this cystoscopy --

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:45.567
this was last week, I went.

00:04:45.567 --> 00:04:46.734
'Cause I got nervous.

00:04:46.734 --> 00:04:48.734
I'm like, "Well, maybe
it's been too long."

00:04:48.734 --> 00:04:51.267
And I went in, and, like,
I don't use drugs

00:04:51.267 --> 00:04:55.834
recreationally, and so when
they give me drugs, it's wild.

00:04:55.834 --> 00:04:58.901
Like, I'm like, "We should
do this all the time!"

00:04:58.901 --> 00:05:00.133
You know what I mean?
[ Laughter ]

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:01.934
Like, it was -- and then --
-You got into it.

00:05:01.934 --> 00:05:04.634
-Yeah, I got so into it,
and I was like, I said to

00:05:04.634 --> 00:05:06.801
the anesthesiologist
before I went under,

00:05:06.801 --> 00:05:09.501
I go, like "What is --
what is this?"

00:05:09.501 --> 00:05:10.934
And he goes "It's propofol."

00:05:10.934 --> 00:05:14.334
And I go, "Oh, isn't the one
that killed Michael Jackson?"

00:05:14.334 --> 00:05:15.968
And he goes "And Joan Rivers."

00:05:15.968 --> 00:05:17.501
And I was like --
[ Laughter ]

00:05:17.501 --> 00:05:20.000
I was like, "This isn't, like,
a 'yes and' situation."

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.467
[ Laughter ]

00:05:22.467 --> 00:05:26.200
"This isn't, like, a brainstorm
of who died doing

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:28.000
the thing you're about to do."

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.868
-No! Stop -- stop saying
those things.

00:05:30.868 --> 00:05:32.501
Oh, my God.
-I wanted them to be, like,

00:05:32.501 --> 00:05:34.567
"Yeah, but there were a lot
of other circumstances.

00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:36.567
He had, like, an amusement park
in his yard,"

00:05:36.567 --> 00:05:37.601
you know what I mean?

00:05:37.601 --> 00:05:40.000
[ Laughter ]

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.067
-But instead, but how --

00:05:42.067 --> 00:05:45.634
-So then I did it, and then I
was all clear, no cancer.

00:05:45.634 --> 00:05:47.634
-Good.
-Yeah, which was great.

00:05:47.634 --> 00:05:49.100
Thanks, thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:50.234
No, no, truly.

00:05:50.234 --> 00:05:52.501
And then I came out,
and I was so high,

00:05:52.501 --> 00:05:55.501
and I'm saying to my wife,
I'm just like,

00:05:55.501 --> 00:05:58.000
"We should do this at home."
You know what I mean?

00:05:58.000 --> 00:05:59.033
Like, the propofol.
And --

00:05:59.033 --> 00:06:00.701
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:00.701 --> 00:06:03.601
And we should get the amusement
park, I mean, why not?

00:06:03.601 --> 00:06:04.767
-Yeah, why not?
-If you're gonna do one,

00:06:04.767 --> 00:06:06.133
why not do the other?

00:06:06.133 --> 00:06:09.234
And -- I was like --
-We do this at home.

00:06:09.234 --> 00:06:12.934
-I was like, I became, like,
the guy at the music festival

00:06:12.934 --> 00:06:14.868
who's just like,
"It's natural, bro,"

00:06:14.868 --> 00:06:17.267
you know what I mean?
Like, "you can do it."

00:06:17.267 --> 00:06:20.400
I was, like, I literally said --
I go, "It's lipids.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:21.868
It's just made of lipids."

00:06:21.868 --> 00:06:24.501
I don't even know
what lipids are, but I --

00:06:24.501 --> 00:06:25.701
[ Laughter ]

00:06:25.701 --> 00:06:27.634
I'd ask the anesthesiologist,

00:06:27.634 --> 00:06:28.801
and he's like "It's lipids."

00:06:28.801 --> 00:06:31.067
And I said to my wife,
I go, "It's lipids.

00:06:31.067 --> 00:06:33.267
Don't worry about it.
We can do this."

00:06:33.267 --> 00:06:34.267
[ Laughter ]

00:06:34.267 --> 00:06:37.467
And like, "It can't kill you."

00:06:37.467 --> 00:06:39.567
And the anesthesiologist goes,
"Yeah, it can."

00:06:39.567 --> 00:06:41.734
[ Laughter ]

00:06:41.734 --> 00:06:45.067
Anyway, I got so high,
and I think I'm still high.

00:06:45.067 --> 00:06:47.267
I put on your suit. I mean --
[ Laughter ]

00:06:47.267 --> 00:06:48.367
-And you look fantastic.
-How does it look?

00:06:48.367 --> 00:06:50.033
-Look at Mike Birbiglia.

00:06:50.033 --> 00:06:53.434
He looks great.
You look fantastic.

00:06:53.434 --> 00:06:55.200
That's what a man
on a Broadway does.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:56.534
Mike Birbiglia, everybody.

00:06:56.534 --> 00:07:00.300
Mike returns to Broadway
with his new show

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:03.901
"The Old Man &amp; The Pool"
starting October 28th.

